    "body": "An Australian Army dozer embarks a Landing Craft Mechanized (LCM-8) at Cowley beach training area in Northern Queensland in preparation for Exercise Keris Woomera 2026. (Photo: Australian Department of Defence)\nSINGAPORE — The Australian army’s recently created Littoral Manoeuvre Group is the correct move for the country’s armed forces, experts told Breaking Defense, especially as tensions continue in the Indo-Pacific.\nAustralia announced plans to transform its ground forces into a littoral-focused force in its 2024 National Defence Strategy , which called for an army “optimised for littoral manoeuvre with a long range land and maritime strike capability.”\nIn March, Australian Army established the Littoral Manoeuvre Group to strengthen the service’s “ability to move, deploy and support land forces across coastal and island regions.”\nBut as budget cuts to some programs loom to make room for AUKUS spending , experts warn that the unit needs to be adequately resourced to ensure it can fulfil that role.\nIn the wake of increased spending on commitments for the trilateral agreement, the country is conducting a budget review to free up funds for the acquisition of nuclear submarines, even as the country is pledging to bring defense spending up to three percent of its gross domestic product in the coming years.\nLast month, the Littoral Manoeuvre Group completed a large-scale amphibious exercise during a training event with Indonesia.\nJohn Blaxland, a professor of international security and intelligence studies at Australian National University, told Breaking Defense that an exercis e like that suggests that Australia envisages working with allies and partners to deploy forces to observe and safeguard key littoral sites, as well as control maritime chokepoints in a conflict.\n“The Littoral Manoeuvre Group is a radical organisational design concept for Australia which reflects a significant reorientation towards being able to rapidly deploy and support forces operating over considerable distances alongside partners,” Blaxland said, adding that the group harks back to World War II, when Australian forces demonstrated mastery of combined arms warfare in the littorals.\nIndependent Australian naval commentator Alex Luck agreed, calling the focus on littoral operations “a step in the right direction.”\n“Australia faces a difficult geographic challenge in its near abroad, in light of a rising Chinese military capability expanding into the wider Pacific and Indian Ocean theatres. Therefore the Australian Army will have to rely on significant naval mobility allowing for flexible coverage of maritime spaces including islands directly under Australian control and maritime approaches around Australian territory,” he added.\nHe also stressed the need to work with Australia’s neighbors and partners such as the US and New Zealand “in securing maritime features around Australia, but particularly across Pacific Island spaces.”\nThe Littoral Manoeuvre Group will include three landing craft battalions, each operating four medium and two heavy landing craft. Both Blaxland and Luck agree that outfitting the group with these landing craft is crucial.\nAustralia awarded contracts to Austal Australia for Land Craft Medium in December 2025 and for Landing Craft Heavy in February. LCM will have a cargo capacity of 80 tonnes (88 tons ) while the LCH will be able to transport 200 soldiers and six M1 Abrams tanks or nine Redback infantry vehicles.\nLuck said the introduction of the two new classes of landing craft would introduce capabilities that the Australian Army has not operated in recent times.\n“The LCH in particular, in conjunction with the Royal Australian Navy’s own amphibious assets, represents an unprecedented new resource the Australian Army will need time to further refine and develo p,” he said.\n“It is therefore important to apply a step by step approach maturing these new amphibious capabilities to allow for further refinement, including allocation of additional resources further strengthening the new LMG concept,” he continued.\nAnd while Australia’s plans to equip the new group as outlined in Australia’s defense Integrated Investment Program appear to be sufficient, Blaxland cautioned that those plans would take time to come to fruition and was dependent on whether funding is available to follow through.\n“The plan appears to be to expand significantly to help move whole battalion groups and supporting elements to work alongside partner forces in the ‘littorals’ across the archipelago. But with significant funding constraints, this is not likely to reach full operational capacity for some years yet,” he said.\nBreaking Defense Video\nA Pentagon impeachment showdown, plus defense funding in limbo\nWatch Now »\npresented by\nThe push to give dismounted forces direct access to LEO and MEO networks [Sponsored]\nWatch Now »\nThe Pentagon wants a massive new military base. Where will it go?\nWatch Now »\nWhat now? Budget fight, Hegseth impeachment on deck for defense-focused lawmakers after midterms\nWatch Now »\nThe defense show circuit keeps rolling, plus Jimmy Stewart goes to war\nWatch Now »\nQatar’s domestic weapons, Turkey’s drone arsenal and the Mecca pact’s next test\nWatch Now »\n3 congressional races that matter for defense\nWatch Now »\nHow General Atomics views Europe’s unmanned future\nWatch Now »\npresented by\nA more flexible way to defend maneuver forces from drones that doesn’t tie them down [Sponsored]\nWatch Now »\nWhat’s haunting the Pentagon this Halloween?\nWatch Now »\nScroll for more video",
